**Writing a data fetch — the pattern you'll use next session.** A very common effect is fetching data when a component first loads. The shape is always the same: an `async` function, `await` the request, and a `try/catch` so a failed network call shows an error instead of crashing the app.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  async function load() {
    try {
      const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/items')
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Request failed')
      const data = await res.json()
      setItems(data)
    } catch (err) {
      setError('Could not load data')
    }
  }
  load()
}, [])
```

- `async`/`await` lets you write the request top-to-bottom instead of nesting callbacks. `await` pauses inside `load()` until the request finishes.
- `res.ok` is `false` for errors like 404 — `fetch` does *not* throw on those by itself, so you check it and `throw` yourself.
- `res.json()` parses the response body into a JavaScript object or array.
- `try/catch` catches a failed request (no network, bad URL, the `throw` above) so you can show a friendly message instead of a blank screen.

The exact same `async` + `await fetch` + `try/catch` shape works inside an event handler too (e.g. a search button) — you'll write it from scratch in the next build session.
